     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-12 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Brewing-inspired method boosts bio-based chemical production</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers from the Sustainable Commodity Chemicals through Enzyme Engineering and Design project have developed a two-step method to manufacture chemicals without harming production microbes. By separating the microbial growth phase from the chemical conversion phase, the team successfully increased styrene production by fivefold compared to traditional techniques.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/brewing-inspired-method-boosts-bio-based-chemical-production.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This approach draws on industrial brewing logic, where microbes produce biocompatible substances like ethanol. By using engineered enzymes to process intermediates into final chemicals, the system prevents toxicity issues that currently limit the biological production of plastics, lubricants, and dyes. The team is now exploring applications for other commodity chemicals including alpha-olefins and FDCA.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Effectively captures the methodology and industrial context of the SuCCEED project.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-brewery-chemical-industry.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
