     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-11-12 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>ED attaches Rs 2.78 crore assets of former Kolkata cop in land-grab case</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Enforcement Directorate</span> <span>#Kolkata Police</span> <span>#Land Grab</span> <span>#Corruption</span></div>
       <p>The Enforcement Directorate (ED) on Thursday provisionally attached immovable assets worth Rs 2.78 crore belonging to Santanu Sinha Biswas, a former Kolkata Police deputy commissioner. Officials informed news agency Press Trust of India that the attachment is part of an ongoing money laundering and corruption case involving alleged strongman Sona Pappu. The agency identified these assets as proceeds of crime, specifying that they include three flats in Kolkata and a plot of land in Kandi in Murshidabad district, held by Biswas, his family members, and associates.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/ed-attaches-rs-278-crore-assets-of-former-kolkata-cop-in-lan.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Biswas previously held several significant positions within the Kolkata Police during the former Trinamool Congress government. Notably, he served as the officer-in-charge of Kalighat police station, an area that includes former chief minister Mamata Banerjee's residence. The ED arrested Biswas in May in connection with an alleged money laundering case linked to a land-grabbing racket, and he remains incarcerated.</p><p>The ED's investigation has revealed that the proceeds of crime were generated through a range of illegal activities. These include extortion, the grabbing of real estate properties, and unauthorized building construction, all facilitated through entities controlled by Biswajit Poddar, also known as Sona Pappu, along with Jay S Kamdar and their associates, as stated by the agency in a PTI-quoted statement. Biswas is accused of receiving financial benefits amounting to several crores of rupees from private entities and businessmen in exchange for extending illegal favours during his tenure in office.</p><p>During his time as the officer-in-charge of Kalighat, Biswas reportedly developed a close association with Jay S Kamdar, assisting him in illegal activities. The ED statement highlighted that Kamdar &quot;enjoyed massive clout within the police establishment through his association with Biswas.&quot; The agency further added that Biswas &quot;directly and indirectly acquired, concealed and possessed proceeds of crime to the tune of at least Rs 2.89 crore.&quot; Separately, the Kolkata Police has registered its own case against Biswas under the Prevention of Corruption Act.</p><p>Biswas is accused of colluding with Sona Pappu and businessman Jay Kamdar in an alleged racket that illegally occupied numerous valuable plots of land across Kolkata over an extended period. Allegations suggest that Biswas leveraged his position in the police force to intimidate and harass vulnerable landowners, coercing them into surrendering their properties. The ED claims this alleged syndicate grabbed land in various parts of Kolkata, generating proceeds of crime worth at least Rs 50 crore.</p><p>In July, the ED filed a charge sheet in the land-grabbing case, presenting evidence that directly implicated Biswas in the misappropriation of approximately Rs 3 crore. The latest action involving assets worth Rs 2.78 crore represents a continuation of the ED's extensive investigation into allegations of money laundering, corruption, and land grabbing against Biswas and his alleged associates.</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing ED investigation and potential further attachments in the land-grab case.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a thorough and accurate report based on the provided source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
