     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-12 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Euclid Space Telescope Releases First Major 3D Cosmic Map</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Euclid Consortium and the European Space Agency have released the 'DR1 Foundation' data, the first major collection from the Euclid space telescope. Covering an area equivalent to 9,500 full moons, the release includes observations of over 900 million galaxies and 60 million quasar candidates, providing a foundation for a comprehensive 3D map of the cosmos.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/euclid-space-telescope-releases-first-major-3d-cosmic-map.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This data set, compiled from 13 months of mission activity, marks a significant milestone in investigating dark matter and dark energy. German research facilities, including the Max Planck Institutes for Astronomy and Extraterrestrial Physics, contributed critical optical components for the telescope's instruments, enabling the unprecedented capture of visible and near-infrared light from across the universe.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Solid summary of the Euclid data release; captures key figures and institutional contributors without filler.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-euclid-3d-universe-date.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
