       <h1>Four Killed in Turkish Military Helicopter Crash</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A Turkish military S-70 helicopter crashed on Thursday evening in Samankoy, located on the outskirts of Malatya, killing all four personnel on board. The Turkish Defence Ministry confirmed the accident, though the cause remains undetermined. Emergency responders, including police and firefighters, were dispatched to the site, where wreckage ignited a fire that spread to a nearby house; no ground casualties were reported.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/four-killed-in-turkish-military-helicopter-crash.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Sikorsky S-70 is a widely utilized transport helicopter, frequently used for military operations. The Turkish Armed Forces maintain a significant presence in the region, including the 7th Main Jet Base located north of the crash site in Malatya.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report is accurate and covers all essential details, including the location, casualty count, and the status of the investigation.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/turkiye-samankoy-army-helicopter-crash-personnel-killed/article71695300.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
