     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Ars Technica · 2026-11-12 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Illicit Synthetic Testosterone Use Leads to Heart Failure</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A Massachusetts man suffered years of recurring heart failure and multiple hospitalizations due to the long-term use of illicit synthetic testosterone derivatives. One of the anabolic-androgenic steroids (AAS) used by the patient was a veterinary-grade drug primarily intended to increase the muscle mass of cows. The case, detailed in the New England Journal of Medicine, highlights the severe health risks associated with such substances.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/illicit-synthetic-testosterone-use-leads-to-heart-failure.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Doctors emphasized that the patient’s initial failure to disclose his drug use hindered accurate diagnosis. Medical professionals warn that similar life-threatening cases are becoming increasingly common, necessitating greater awareness among clinicians to identify and address the dangerous physical effects of illicit performance-enhancing substances.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is concise and captures the essence of the NEJM report and the physician's warning regarding illicit steroid use.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://arstechnica.com/health/2026/11/mans-secret-use-of-synthetic-testosterone-for-cows-nearly-destroyed-his-heart/" rel="noopener">Ars Technica</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
