     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-11-12 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Dominates Afghanistan in U19 Asia Cup Semi-Final</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#U19</span> <span>#Asia Cup</span> <span>#India</span></div>
       <p>India established early dominance against Afghanistan in the U19 Asia Cup semi-final at the Al-Amerat Cricket Ground in Oman, securing wickets at regular intervals to dent the opposition's innings. The initial breakthrough came from Ishan Sood, who, sharing new-ball duties with Kavya, dismissed Abidullah Nadarjan for 14 runs off 23 balls, including two boundaries. Nadarjan feathered a faint edge through to Anvay Dravid, the wicketkeeper, after attempting a booming cover drive, bringing Afghanistan to 29/1 after 6.5 overs. Kavya Patel then accounted for Faridullah Ihsas, who chopped on for 13 runs off 29 balls after trying to push a good-length delivery off the back foot. This dismissal, occurring at 51/2 after 12.5 overs, broke a developing partnership that had begun to stabilize after the first wicket.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/india-dominates-afghanistan-in-u19-asia-cup-semi-final.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Indian attack continued to apply pressure, with Yashbardhan Chauhan, the vice-captain, delivering a brilliant spell that included a maiden over and contained Afghanistan's scoring. Chauhan secured a crucial wicket by dismissing Subhan Zakhail for 31 runs off 44 balls. Subhan's back-foot punch resulted in the ball hitting the bat, then the pads, and deflecting onto the stumps, an important wicket for India that saw Afghanistan fall to 86/3 after 19.4 overs. Earlier, Subhan had shown aggression, hitting a six off Manal and a boundary off Kavya Patel, contributing to Afghanistan reaching 60/2 after 14.5 overs. Meanwhile, Noman Salarzai also provided some resistance, hitting the first six of the innings off Ulva, pushing the score to 70/2 after 17.2 overs.</p><p>A significant partnership then emerged between Noman Salarzai and Shah Faisal Rahimi for the fourth wicket, adding 57 runs and bringing some stability to Afghanistan's innings. Noman Salarzai reached his half-century in 74 balls, marking a milestone that saw the partnership also cross 50 runs, positioning Afghanistan at 139/3 after 34.2 overs. Despite a near-fatal mix-up between Noman and Faisal, where a direct hit was needed, and an earlier LBW appeal against Noman that the umpire ruled hit the gloves first, the pair managed to build their stand. However, India's fielders remained sharp, with Manal Chauhan making an excellent effort at backward point to prevent a certain boundary at 17/0 (4).</p><p>The momentum shifted decisively back to India with a rapid succession of wickets. Arjun Rajput broke the resilient 57-run partnership by dismissing Noman Salarzai for 51 runs off 78 balls, caught by Yashbardhan Chauhan in the deep at long-off, with Afghanistan at 143/4 after 36 overs. This crucial breakthrough was immediately followed by Aryan Savsani striking twice in the same over. His impactful spell included the dismissal of Amir Hamza, who, promoted in the batting order, departed for a one-ball duck after top-edging a wild swing to captain Lakshya Raichandani at cover. These quick blows saw Afghanistan lose three wickets in the space of two balls, plummeting to 144/5 after 36.2 overs and then six wickets down, firmly turning the game in India's favour.</p><p>What to watch: India's continued bowling performance in the semi-final.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a comprehensive and accurate summary of the provided live coverage, successfully incorporating specific player statistics and match events.</p>
