     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-11-12 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Kerala Woman Loses Rs 49.2 Lakh to Fake Nirmala Sitharaman Investment Video</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cyber Crime</span> <span>#Nirmala Sitharaman</span> <span>#Fraud</span> <span>#Kerala</span></div>
       <p>A 45-year-old woman from Kerala allegedly lost Rs 49.2 lakh in an online trading fraud, after being lured by a fake Facebook video featuring Union Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman, police reported. The Kozhikode Rural Cyber Crime Police registered a case on Wednesday following the woman's complaint that the fraudsters had neither returned her investment nor paid the promised profits, according to news agency PTI.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/kerala-woman-loses-rs-492-lakh-to-fake-nirmala-sitharaman-in.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The complainant, a resident of Maikkavu in Thamarassery, encountered the deceptive video on Facebook, which purportedly showed Ms. Sitharaman claiming that online trading could generate high returns, police told PTI. Believing these claims, the woman clicked on a link accompanying the video and proceeded to invest as instructed by the fraudsters.</p><p>Between July 15 and September 22 of this year, she transferred a total of Rs 49.2 lakh from her accounts with the State Bank of India and Puthuppadi Service Cooperative Bank to various bank accounts provided by the accused, police said. After the accused failed to return the money or pay the promised profits, the woman approached the police, leading to the registration of a case under sections of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita and the Information Technology Act.</p><p>Police are currently tracking the bank accounts to which the money was transferred and are attempting to freeze them. Authorities noted that AI-generated videos featuring Union ministers promoting investment schemes have been circulating on social media platforms, with repeated advisories warning the public against falling prey to such cyber frauds.</p><p>What to watch: Police efforts to trace and freeze the fraudulent bank accounts.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately captures all key details from the source, including the timeline, the specific banks involved, and the nature of the fraud.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/kerala/story/kerala-online-trading-fraud-fake-nirmala-sitharaman-video-rs-49-2-lakh-ptag-3037756-2026-11-12?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-11-12/kerala-woman-loses-rs-492-lakh-to-fake-nirmala-sitharaman-in" data-title="Kerala Woman Loses Rs 49.2 Lakh to Fake Nirmala Sitharaman Investment Video"><b>Share:</b>
         <a class="sh-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg class="ic" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M8 1.8A6.2 6.2 0 0 0 2.7 11L1.8 14.2l3.3-.9A6.2 6.2 0 1 0 8 1.8z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>WhatsApp</a> ·
