     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-11-12 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Microsoft Adds 25 Original Xbox Games to PC</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Microsoft has expanded its game preservation initiative, allowing users to play 25 original Xbox titles on Windows PCs. The titles are playable via an emulator, though physical discs are incompatible with the program. While most games require individual digital purchases, six titles, including Morrowind and Conker: Live and Reloaded, are currently included with Xbox Game Pass.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/microsoft-adds-25-original-xbox-games-to-pc.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Microsoft is also introducing achievements to select titles, including the Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic series, playable on PC and consoles. This rollout follows a previous four-game launch in July, with further expansions expected as the company works toward its goal of broader cross-generational compatibility.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the game preservation initiative and the specific details regarding achievements and Game Pass availability.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/games/1032321/microsoft-xbox-original-games-pc-backwards-compatibility" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
