     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-12 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Molecular fabric innovation improves industrial chemical separation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Scientists from UT Austin and Zhejiang University have developed a molecularly woven fabric to efficiently separate cyclohexane from benzene. This process is critical for producing industrial materials like nylon and detergents, which currently rely on energy-intensive distillation due to the chemicals' similar boiling points.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/molecular-fabric-innovation-improves-industrial-chemical-sep.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The fabric utilizes chiral molecular networks that interlock in a spiral pattern. By stacking these layers, researchers created a filter that selectively separates the chemicals. This innovation aims to reduce the significant energy consumption associated with traditional separation methods, providing a more practical and sustainable solution for the chemical manufacturing industry.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise and accurate summary of the research and its industrial potential.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-reusable-molecular-fabric-energy-industrial.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
