     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-11-12 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NITI Aayog: PLI Schemes Meet 28% Job Target by FY26, Urges Sector-Specific and Demand-Side Focus</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#PLI Schemes</span> <span>#NITI Aayog</span> <span>#Employment</span> <span>#Manufacturing</span></div>
       <p>India’s production-linked incentive (PLI) schemes have achieved only 28 percent of their targeted employment generation by 2025-26 (FY26), creating 849,069 jobs against a target of 3 million. This finding comes from a first-of-its-kind assessment by NITI Aayog, highlighting an uneven translation of the government’s manufacturing push into job creation. Despite this, investment mobilisation has reached 76 percent of the envisaged target, amounting to around ₹2.4 trillion against a ₹3.15 trillion goal. Production, meanwhile, achieved approximately ₹22.66 trillion, or 64 percent of the ₹35.38 trillion target, by FY26.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/niti-aayog-pli-schemes-meet-28-job-target-by-fy26-urges-sect.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Aayog's report, titled &quot;Impact Assessment of PLI Schemes&quot; and released on Thursday, noted that the 14 PLI schemes, launched between 2020 and 2021 with tenures ranging from three to 10 years, have completed 58 percent of their aggregate implementation period. This accounts for 578 months elapsed out of a total duration of 996 months, with a substantial 42 percent of the aggregate period still remaining. The report called for refining the design of the schemes, stating that the focus must shift from assessing initial success to strengthening long-term effectiveness and delivering deeper, more sustained industrial transformation.</p><p>Annual employment performance has shown significant variation, according to the report. Employment generation stood at 86 percent of the target up to 2022-23, around 85 percent in 2023-24, and surged to 156 percent in 2024-25, before falling to 31 percent of the annual target in 2025-26. The think tank explained that the divergence between cumulative employment and investment and production must be understood in the context of the gestation period of manufacturing investments. Investment typically occurs before facilities are fully operational, production ramps up as capacity is commissioned, and employment evolves further as operations reach commercial scale.</p><p>The assessment identified PLI schemes in four sectors as &quot;on track&quot; based on investment, production, exports, and employment: large-scale electronics manufacturing, pharmaceutical drugs, telecommunications and networking products, and food products. Conversely, the report found weak or partial impacts in specific areas, such as the bulk drugs PLI scheme on exports, the medical devices scheme on employment, and the Electronics/Technology Products (IT Hardware 2.0) scheme on investments. Ishtiyaque Ahmed, a programme director at the think tank, commented that &quot;70 per cent of players and indicators are on track,&quot; indicating overall success despite variations.</p><p>NITI Aayog Chief Executive Officer Anurag Jain stated that schemes requiring changes would be reviewed in coordination with the concerned line ministries to address identified issues. The Aayog emphasized that while some sectors have responded strongly with visible increases in investment, production, employment, and exports, others have made relatively modest progress. This variation is often linked to differences in technology adoption, gestation periods, global competition, and capital requirements, necessitating a sector-specific understanding and a move beyond a uniform approach in policy design.</p><p>The report also suggested that the next generation of PLI schemes should incorporate demand-pull measures. It noted that the current scheme is largely supply-driven, encouraging firms to increase production and build capacity through financial incentives linked to output. For long-term sustainability, strong demand-side support is crucial. This could include stronger domestic procurement frameworks, export market development, strategic government purchasing, and policies that stimulate consumption of domestically produced high-value goods. The Aayog concluded that policy evaluation must remain patient and realistic, avoiding premature judgments, given that the full effects on production, employment, and exports often emerge only after a significant lag, especially in sectors with advanced technologies and long gestation periods.</p><p>What to watch: Future policy refinements and sector-specific interventions for PLI schemes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent work synthesizing a complex report, clearly presenting the statistics, the Aayog's analysis, and the proposed future policy shifts.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/pli-schemes-meet-just-28-of-employment-target-by-fy26-niti-aayog-report-126111201783_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
