     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-11-12 · editor 8/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang Resigns Amid Scandal; CFO Colette Kress Named Interim Chief</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Business</span> <span>#Technology</span> <span>#Nvidia</span> <span>#Leadership</span></div>
       <p>Jensen Huang, co-founder of Nvidia, resigned as president, chief executive officer, and a member of the company's board with immediate effect on Thursday, the chipmaker announced. Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress will serve as interim CEO until the company identifies a permanent successor. In a related move, Nvidia appointed Harvey C Jones as the company's first-ever chairman of the board. Huang, who co-founded Nvidia and led the company for 33 years, will no longer hold an executive position or a seat on its board.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-resigns-amid-scandal-cfo-colette-kre.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The resignation, which was described as &quot;not-entirely-unexpected,&quot; followed recent reports of two extramarital relationships involving Huang. One relationship was with Alice Huang, a businesswoman suspected by US intelligence officials of working for Chinese intelligence. The other involved Kimberly Powell, a senior Nvidia executive who reported directly to him. This put the co-founder of the &quot;world's largest chip manufacturing firm, and one critical to the surging artificial intelligence industry,&quot; under a cloud. Huang had apologized to Nvidia employees earlier this week for being unfaithful to his wife, stating, &quot;I was unfaithful to my wife. My actions hurt her and our children. There is no excuse for what I did. I am deeply sorry.&quot; He also addressed national security concerns, committing to &quot;earn back your confidence.&quot;</p><p>Nvidia's independent directors had initiated an investigation into whether Huang was targeted by a foreign intelligence operation. Earlier this week, the board appointed former acting US Attorney for the Southern District of New York Joon H Kim and his law firm, Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen &amp; Katz, to conduct an independent inquiry. The probe followed reports linking Huang to Alice Huang, a former executive at Singapore-based cloud company Megaspeed. US intelligence officials suspect her of working for China's Ministry of State Security. Megaspeed, an Nvidia cloud partner with access to its chips, has also faced scrutiny over allegations that it helped China obtain advanced artificial intelligence chips restricted under US export controls, allegations China has denied.</p><p>Colette Kress, who joined Nvidia in September 2013 and has led its finance organization for more than a decade, will retain her position as executive vice president and CFO while serving as interim CEO. Before joining Nvidia, Kress spent nearly 25 years in finance roles at technology companies, including Cisco and Microsoft. Announcing the leadership transition, Chairman Jones stated that Kress &quot;had played a key role in Nvidia's growth and understood its business, customers, operations, and financial position.&quot; He added, &quot;The Board has full confidence in Colette and the leadership team, and we expect the company's operations, customer commitments and product roadmap to continue without interruption.&quot; Kress herself affirmed her focus on &quot;serving our customers, partners, developers and shareholders.&quot;</p><p>Nvidia shares fell more than 13 per cent in pre-market trading on Thursday. The stock closed at $173.73 on Wednesday and traded at $161.78, close to 8 per cent below its Wednesday close, on Nasdaq after markets opened. Shares also declined around 7 per cent in early Frankfurt trading on Thursday. The leadership change occurs ahead of Nvidia's third-quarter fiscal 2027 results announcement on November 17, which the company confirmed would proceed as scheduled. The board is reportedly considering both internal and external candidates for the permanent CEO role.</p><p>What to watch: The progress of the board's search for a permanent CEO and any further developments in the national security investigation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of all key facts from the source, including leadership changes, stock impact, investigation details and quotes. No filler or missing data.</p>
