      <a href="/feed.xml" title="RSS">RSS</a>
      <button id="tdp-theme" title="Toggle light/dark">Light</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <nav class="catrow"><span class="catgrp"><b>News</b><a href="/category/national">national</a><a href="/category/world">world</a><a href="/category/defence">defence</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Money</b><a href="/category/business">business</a><a href="/category/finance">finance</a><a href="/category/education">education</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Sports</b><a href="/category/sports">sports</a><a href="/category/cricket">cricket</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Culture</b><a href="/category/entertainment">entertainment</a><a href="/category/positive">positive</a><a href="/category/funny">funny</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Life</b><a href="/category/science">science</a><a href="/category/environment">environment</a><a href="/category/space">space</a><a href="/category/health">health</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Tech</b><a href="/category/tech">tech</a><a href="/category/ai">ai</a></span></nav></div><div class="gtr"><span>Read in your language:</span><div id="google_translate_element"></div></div><div class="wrap"><main id="content"><p class="crumb"><a href="/">← Front page</a> · <a href="/archive">Archive</a> · <a href="/category/world">world</a></p>
     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-11-12 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>President Xi Reiterates Call for Taiwan 'Reunification,' Citing Unstoppable Historical Tide</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#China</span> <span>#Taiwan</span> <span>#Xi Jinping</span> <span>#G7</span></div>
       <p>Chinese President Xi Jinping asserted on Thursday, November 12, 2026, that the &quot;tide of history cannot be stopped&quot; and that the &quot;complete reunification of the motherland must be realised, and it will certainly be realised&quot; with Taiwan. Speaking in Beijing's Great Hall of the People, Mr. Xi urged complete &quot;reunification&quot; of Taiwan with the mainland, pledging to do so peacefully while notably omitting any mention of the use of force, according to The Hindu World.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/president-xi-reiterates-call-for-taiwan-reunification-citing.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>China continues to view democratically governed Taiwan as its own territory, a claim Taipei strongly rejects. Beijing has consistently offered a &quot;one country, two systems&quot; model of autonomy for the island, a proposal not supported by any major Taiwanese political party. Mr. Xi underscored China's adherence to &quot;peaceful reunification and the policy of 'one country, two systems',&quot; expressing willingness to engage in talks with Taiwan while upholding the &quot;one-China principle,&quot; Beijing's stance that both sides of the Taiwan Strait constitute one country.</p><p>Despite the absence of explicit threats of force in Mr. Xi's speech, China has never renounced the use of force for Taiwan and maintains a daily military presence around the island, extending its operational reach further into the Pacific. The G7 Foreign Ministers have previously stressed the critical importance of maintaining peace and stability across the Taiwan Strait, noting that China's actions east of Taiwan pose a threat to regional stability.</p><p>Mr. Xi's remarks were delivered during a speech commemorating the 160th birthday of revolutionary hero Sun Yat-sen, who played a pivotal role in the 1911 revolt that ended centuries of imperial rule and established the Republic of China. The Chinese President called upon all Chinese, including those in Taiwan, to unite against &quot;Taiwan independence 'separatist' forces&quot; and strive for the peaceful development of cross-Strait ties. Taiwan's government did not provide an immediate response to these comments.</p><p>Recent tensions highlight the ongoing friction. Taiwan's Coast Guard reported &quot;unusually large&quot; numbers of Chinese vessels participating in coordinated operations around Taiwan's main island and the disputed Pratas island. Taiwan stated it drove away 16 Chinese ships involved in what it termed &quot;maritime harassment,&quot; which it described as a clear expansion of power violating international law and undermining regional peace. Taiwan had also called on its people to avoid China's events marking the Sun anniversary, citing China's &quot;distorted view&quot; of his legacy.</p><p>The speech precedes the APEC summit in Shenzhen, where Mr. Xi is expected to meet U.S. President Donald Trump, and occurs just over two weeks before crucial local elections in Taiwan. Taiwan President Lai Ching-te and his ruling Democratic Progressive Party (DPP) advocate for Taiwan's distinct identity from China, adopting a firmer stance with Beijing. They have expressed particular displeasure over Beijing's attempts to claim credit for the victory in World War Two, asserting that the Republic of China government fought alongside the Allies, predating the People's Republic of China. China, in turn, labels Mr. Lai a &quot;separatist&quot; and has refused dialogue with him, while Mr. Lai maintains that only Taiwan's people can determine their future.</p><p>What to watch: The APEC summit and upcoming Taiwan local elections for further developments.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive and well-structured report that integrates the source text effectively while providing necessary political context.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/chinas-xi-says-taiwan-reunification-must-happen-as-history-cannot-be-stopped/article71692573.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
