     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-12 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Rare Star System GPM J071507−114153 Emits Radio Signals Every Eight Minutes</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Astronomy</span> <span>#Stars</span> <span>#Radio Pulses</span></div>
       <p>Scientists at the Curtin University node of the International Center for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) have identified a rare star system, GPM J071507−114153, as the source of mysterious radio pulses. This newly discovered system consists of an unusual pairing: a dead star, specifically a white dwarf, that spins every eight minutes, and a G-type star similar to our own sun, orbiting each other. The study, titled &quot;A long-period transient with a Sun-like companion,&quot; was published in Nature Astronomy.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/rare-star-system-gpm-j071507114153-emits-radio-signals-every.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This detection marks a significant breakthrough as it is the first time radio pulses have been observed from a white dwarf orbiting a sun-like star. Typically, such pulses are found in systems involving smaller and cooler red dwarf stars. The discovery positions GPM J071507−114153 within a broader class of celestial objects known as long-period transients (LPTs), which emit radio signals that cycle on and off over intervals ranging from minutes to hours. Around a dozen LPTs have been detected since the first was found by the same ICRAR team in 2021, with astronomers still working to fully comprehend their nature.</p><p>Previous LPT systems often contained a white dwarf and another star, but the precise origin of the radio pulses remained ambiguous. Dr. Sam McSweeney, lead author and commissioning scientist with the Square Kilometer Array Observatory, who made the discovery while at Curtin University/ICRAR, clarified this. &quot;This is the first time the pulses are clear enough to see the white dwarf moving in its orbit around its companion, so we know that the pulsations are coming from a region close to the white dwarf, or possibly even from the white dwarf itself,&quot; he stated. The team processed over 100 hours of radio data at the Pawsey Supercomputing Research Center to confirm the white dwarf's orbital motion.</p><p>Beyond pinpointing the pulse source, the radio signals provided additional insights. Associate Professor Natasha Hurley-Walker, a co-author on the paper, noted that the pulses allowed the team to detect stellar flares on the sun-like companion star, akin to those that produce aurorae on Earth. Furthermore, observations revealed the white dwarf moving behind a region with a strong magnetic field, most likely a &quot;stellar prominence&quot;—a large loop of plasma erupting from a star's surface. &quot;It's amazing that by using radio wavelengths we can detect the storms on a star over 5,000 light-years away,&quot; Hurley-Walker commented.</p><p>The discovery utilized several advanced observatories. The system was initially detected in a large survey using the Murchison Widefield Array (MWA), which scanned the entire Milky Way visible from Australia. Subsequently, the MeerKAT radio telescope in South Africa was employed to determine its precise position. Optical observations from the W.M. Keck Observatory on Maunakea, Hawaiʻi Island, then confirmed its association with a G-type star. Both the MWA, led by Curtin University, and MeerKAT, operated by the South African Radio Astronomy Observatory, serve as precursor telescopes for the upcoming SKA telescopes—SKA-Low in Australia and SKA-Mid in South Africa. Dr. McSweeney expressed optimism for future research, remarking, &quot;If we are already making such discoveries with precursor telescopes, just imagine how many more star systems we will be able to find, and what a beautifully detailed view of their signals we will have, once the SKAO telescopes are completely built.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: Further research into long-period transients and enhanced discoveries with the fully constructed SKAO telescopes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: A comprehensive and well-structured report that covers all technical and contextual details provided in the source material, including the specific observatories and researchers involved.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-rare-star-emits-radio-minutes.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
