     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-11-12 · editor 6/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Taiwan Investigates Chinese Woman Linked to Nvidia CEO</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A Taiwanese minister confirmed that Alice Huang, a woman accused of spying for China while involved in an extramarital affair with Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, entered Taiwan eight times between 2024 and 2025. Mainland Affairs Council Minister Chiu Chui-cheng stated that Huang entered using a Hong Kong passport under standard business visitor status without triggering security flags.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/taiwan-investigates-chinese-woman-linked-to-nvidia-ceo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Taiwanese officials noted that they received no intelligence regarding the alleged espionage at the time of her visits. The investigation occurs amidst broader US concerns regarding national security, export controls, and potential data breaches involving Nvidia’s technology in the global semiconductor race.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief claims the US advised Taiwan to monitor Alice Huang as early as 2024, but the source text attributes this claim to a report, and the brief fails to mention the source's date of publication (2026).</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/chinese-woman-accused-of-spying-on-nvidia-chief-entered-taiwan-eight-times/article71692694.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
