     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-12 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>The planet ‘is being killed’, Greta Thunberg tells Cop31 climate summit</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Greta Thunberg</span> <span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#Cop31</span> <span>#Environment</span></div>
       <p>Swedish activist Greta Thunberg delivered a sharp rebuke to world governments at the Cop31 climate summit in Antalya, Turkey, marking her first appearance at the annual UN climate talks in five years. Thunberg, who became famous for heading the school climate strike movement, stated unequivocally, “The planet is not dying, it is being killed and we know by who.” Her message excoriated governments for their persistent failure to prevent environmental breakdown.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/the-planet-is-being-killed-greta-thunberg-tells-cop31-climat.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The 23-year-old activist addressed those gathered at the summit, emphasizing the critical urgency of the climate crisis. Her criticism comes amidst an ongoing international context where countries are perceived as failing to adequately address the escalating environmental challenges. This direct confrontation highlights her long-standing call for more decisive action from global leaders.</p><p>Thunberg's return to the UN climate stage in Antalya serves as a renewed push for accountability. Her remarks underscore a consistent theme in her activism: that the responsibility for environmental degradation lies with those in power who have not taken sufficient steps to avert it. The Cop31 summit continues to be a forum for discussions on these pressing global issues.</p><p>What to watch: Discussions at the Cop31 summit continue in Antalya, Turkey.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately captures the essence and specific quotes from the provided source summary, maintaining a professional tone appropriate for a news report.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
