     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-11-12 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Ukraine Reports New Starlink Disruptions as US Envoys Meet Putin in Moscow</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Ukraine</span> <span>#Russia</span> <span>#Starlink</span> <span>#US Diplomacy</span></div>
       <p>President Volodymyr Zelensky has reported that Ukraine's military is again facing &quot;systematic&quot; restrictions to Starlink's military-grade service, Starshield, hindering efforts to intercept Russian jet-powered drones. Zelensky told European leaders in Dublin that Ukraine was attempting to clarify the situation with the American side, adding that if decisions in America &quot;treat Ukrainian territory as if it is not Ukrainian, then this tells us where things are going.&quot;</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-12/ukraine-reports-new-starlink-disruptions-as-us-envoys-meet-p.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The disruption, which Zelensky stated has been ongoing for several days, is &quot;helping the Russians kill more Ukrainians and carry out more accurate strikes against Ukraine.&quot; Starshield, developed by Elon Musk's SpaceX, is crucial for front-line communication. A similar connectivity issue affected the front line in late October for about a week, with the cause of temporary restrictions unclear and differing explanations from US officials and SpaceX at the time.</p><p>This development coincided with US envoys Jared Kushner and Steve Witkoff meeting Russian President Vladimir Putin in Moscow on Thursday, November 12, 2026. Videos released by Russian media showed the envoys welcomed by Kremlin envoy Kirill Dmitriev. They later met Putin and three senior advisors at the Kremlin, following talks aimed at ending the war in Ukraine that concluded in Abu Dhabi the previous day.</p><p>During their meeting, Putin asked the US envoys to convey his &quot;gratitude&quot; to US First Lady Melania Trump for her involvement in last month's Kherson region ceasefire, which facilitated the evacuation of hundreds of civilians from the Russian-occupied city of Oleshky. The Russian president then proposed a similar five-day ceasefire for Kostyantynivka, a contested front-line Ukrainian city in the eastern Donetsk region. Ukraine is unlikely to accept, disputing Russia's full control of the town and fearing a Russian push to seize it during a truce.</p><p>The separate in-person meetings with the Russian and Ukrainian presidents were announced on Wednesday, following two days of talks in Abu Dhabi focused on Europe's postwar security architecture. Nearly five years into Russia's full-scale invasion, Moscow maintains maximalist demands for sovereign Ukrainian territory and for Western countries to abandon future commitments to Kyiv's protection.</p><p>Putin reiterated on Thursday that the presence of foreign troops in Ukraine was &quot;unacceptable&quot; and expressed Russia's desire for Americans to engage with Europe &quot;so that the Europeans can decide what they truly want: peace or war.&quot; European nations, having previously cut communications with the Kremlin, have recently grown concerned about the current US administration's commitment to European security and its perceived softening towards the Kremlin, pushing for a seat at the negotiating table.</p><p>Kushner, who led the Abu Dhabi talks involving only Russia and Ukraine, indicated he would ask Putin to &quot;give his blessing&quot; to invite European representatives to future discussions, particularly concerning the continent's security architecture. He also urged Europe to &quot;identify one person to speak on their behalf&quot; in negotiations. However, Russia has opposed European involvement, citing their support for Kyiv. Vladimir Medinsky, who headed the Russian delegation in Abu Dhabi, stated that all parties had &quot;moved closer to achieving peace,&quot; but &quot;a lot depends on Ukraine, on the position of the United States, and on whether Europe comes to its senses.&quot; Kushner and Witkoff are expected to travel to Dublin to meet Zelensky.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively synthesizes the source material, capturing the key developments regarding Starlink and the diplomatic missions.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwrkgmymk55zo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
